# Every text file is LF, in the repository *and* in the working tree.
#
# `[tool.ruff.format] line-ending = "lf"` makes `ruff format --check` a gate on
# LF, and `core.autocrlf=true` — the Windows default, and set on the machine this
# was developed on — makes every checkout CRLF. The two cannot both hold, and the
# gate loses: measured on a fresh `git worktree add` of HEAD, `workspace.py` had
# 699 CRLF and 0 LF, and `ruff format --check .` reported every file containing a
# Python code block. It is not a formatting mistake anyone made; it is a
# checkout setting nobody could see.
#
# `eol=lf` is the fix rather than `text=auto` alone, because `text=auto` with no
# `eol` means "let `core.autocrlf` decide", which is the setting that is wrong.
#
# `text=auto` and not `text`, so git still decides for itself whether a file is
# text. Nothing tracked today is binary, but the swc binary under
# `apps/web/node_modules` is gitignored rather than absent, and a blanket `text`
# would corrupt it the moment it stopped being ignored.
* text=auto eol=lf
